# Video encoder rung — sourced by gw-keepalive.sh on EVERY gateway start, so switching is
# "edit this file, kill the gateway, the keepalive brings it back on the new rung".
#
# THERE IS ONE KNOB: VIDEO_KBPS, the link budget in kilobits per second.  Everything else the
# encoder takes — quantizers, per-frame cap, settle time, keyframe quantizer, resync interval — is
# derived from it in video-profiles.lisp (RUNG-SETTINGS), and the derived numbers are printed at
# startup as the "[rung]" line.  They are deliberately NOT duplicated here: two copies of one
# derivation is exactly how the old three-profile table drifted out of step with itself.
#
#   THE LADDER      5   10   20   40   80   160   320  kbps
#                0.61 1.22 2.44 4.88 9.77 19.53 39.06  KB/s
#
# WHAT IT COSTS TO START.  A VP8 keyframe at 1280x800 is 22116 bytes at qi 127 — the coarsest
# quantizer the format has — so time-to-first-picture is a division, not a tuning choice.
# Measured on a real desktop capture, encoded here and decoded with ffmpeg:
#
#   kbps   keyframe qi   keyframe   first picture   usable?
#      5           127      22 KB         35 s      no  — half a minute of nothing
#     10           127      22 KB         18 s      no  — same, halved
#     20           127      22 KB          8.8 s    marginal — reading, not working
#     40           127      22 KB          4.4 s    YES — this is the floor
#     80           127      22 KB          2.2 s    comfortable
#    160            96      40 KB          2.0 s    good
#    320            56      81 KB          2.0 s    sharp
#
# THOSE ARE THE FULL-SIZE NUMBERS AND THEY NO LONGER DECIDE ANYTHING, because the first picture is
# no longer sent at full size.  A keyframe's price is a MACROBLOCK COUNT, so a quarter-size one is
# a quarter of the bytes, and the sender now opens every session there and climbs.  Measured, same
# capture, same encoder, median of three runs:
#
#   kbps   first pixels   at        full resolution
#     20        0.67 s    320x200        12.1 s
#     40        0.36 s    320x200         6.3 s
#     80        0.22 s    320x200         3.5 s
#    160        0.21 s    320x200         3.3 s
#    320        0.28 s    320x200         3.9 s
#
# Full resolution arrives one to three seconds later than it used to and pixels arrive thirty to a
# hundred and thirty times sooner.  Nothing on screen for four and a half seconds and then
# everything is a worse trade than something at a third of a second that sharpens as it goes.
#
# Three things make the low rungs behave at all, all of them consequences of those numbers rather
# than preferences:
#
#   * THE PERIODIC KEYFRAME IS NOW RATE-SCALED.  It used to fire every 3 s for the first 30 s of a
#     session — at 40 kbps that is 16 s of channel time per 3 s of wall clock, so the stream could
#     never catch up, and at 5 kbps the session never produced a first image at all.  It now costs
#     at most 8% of the link: ~30 s at 320 kbps, ~7 minutes at 5 kbps.  It cannot be removed
#     outright, because we parse no RTCP (no PLI, no receiver reports) — a viewer that lost sync
#     and has not opened the control channel has no other way back.
#
#   * THE KEYFRAME IS CODED COARSE AND SHARPENED AFTERWARDS.  Every macroblock of a coarse keyframe
#     is marked stale, and the idle refinement pass — which already existed — repaints the screen
#     at the rung's own quantizer, a budgeted frame at a time.  Whole and soft in 4 s beats sharp
#     and half-drawn in 20.
#
#   * THE PER-FRAME CAP COMES FROM THE RATE (1.5 s of budget, floor 4 KB).  A fixed 32 KB frame is
#     51 s in flight at 5 kbps, which is not a latency bound, it is a stall.
#
# Two things TRIED AND REJECTED, with the measurements, so they are not tried again:
#
#   * Splitting the intra coverage across frames — a tiny flat keyframe (2.1 KB), then filling the
#     screen in with inter frames — costs 32 KB for the same picture the 22 KB keyframe gives,
#     because an inter macroblock cannot use intra prediction and every inter frame re-pays ~500
#     bytes of per-macroblock skip flags.  It shows you the top of the screen sooner and the whole
#     screen much later.
#
#   * A FIXED lower resolution.  640x400 makes the keyframe 5.6 KB — 4x cheaper, and genuinely
#     tempting at 5 kbps — and makes desktop text unreadable.  Decoded side by side, full
#     resolution at the COARSEST quantizer keeps headings and body text legible; half resolution at
#     a much finer one does not.  That measurement stands and it is why the ladder has no low-
#     resolution rungs.
#
#     What it does NOT rule out, and what the sender now does, is changing resolution WITH THE
#     DAMAGE.  Illegible text only matters on a screen you are reading, and a screen you are
#     reading is a screen that is not moving.  While it moves there is no legibility to lose — the
#     alternative at 40 kbps is one whole-screen frame every four and a half seconds — and when it
#     stops, full resolution comes back and the idle pass sharpens it.  Local damage never leaves
#     full resolution at all, because the macroblocks that did not change are already correct in
#     the viewer's decoder and cost one bit each to keep.  See RESOLUTION FOLLOWS DAMAGE in
#     webrtc-media's vp8-rtp.lisp for the thresholds and the hysteresis.
#
# Below ~40 kbps prefer the RFB path (?video=0): a scroll there costs one CopyRect strip and the
# text is lossless.  See "Video or RFB?" in DEPLOY.md.

export VIDEO_KBPS=40
